:XstFindMemoryMatch()
##################################
#####  XstFindMemoryMatch()  #####
##################################

	addrMinMatch = XstFindMemoryMatch (@addrStart, addrPast, addrMatch, @minMatchLength, maxMatchLength)
	
	input
	@addrStart      = address of memory to search - first byte
	addrPast        = address of memory to search - last byte plus one
	addrMatch       = address of string to search for - first byte
	@minMatchLength = minimum number of bytes that must match
	maxMatchLength  = maximum number of bytes that may match (length of match string)
	
	output
	@addrStart      = address of memory to search - first byte that matched
	addrPast        = unchanged
	addrMatch       = unchanged
	@minMatchLength = number of characters that matched
	maxMatchLength  = unchanged
	addrMinMatch    = zero if no-minimum match or address of first byte that matched
	
	
	EXTERNAL FUNCTION  XstFindMemoryMatch (addrStart, addrPast, addrString, minMatchLength, maxMatchLength)
	
:XstStringToNumber()
#################################
#####  XstStringToNumber()  #####
#################################

	specType = XstStringToNumber (@value$, start, @after, @rtype, @value$$)
	
	Convert all or part of a string into a number of natural data type.
	
	  specType   = explicit type  ( or -1 for numeric format error )
	  start      = starting offset in value$  ( not modified )
	  after      = returned with offset after last numeric character
	  rtype      = returned with "natural data type" of value
	  value$$    = returned with value of number in rtype format
	
	XstStringToNumber() converts all or part of value$ into a numeric
	value.  It returns the numeric value in value$$, its natural type
	in rtype, and any explicit type in specType.  value$ can be
	passed by reference for faster execution.
	
	XstStringToNumber() scans value$ from offset startOff, skips
	leading whitespace and unprintable characters, then converts
	subsequent characters into a number.
	
	If the first of the subsequent characters cannot begin a valid
	number, XstStringToNumber() returns specType=-1, rtype=0, and
	the offset of the bad character in afterOff.
	
	XstStringToNumber() collects characters until it encounters one
	that is not a valid part of a number.  It returns the offset of
	this character in afterOff, the natural type of the number in
	rtype, and the value of the number in value$$.
	
	value$$ is a GIANT number, but the numeric value stored in
	value$$ is not in GIANT format unless rtype=$$GIANT.
	
	rtype is always SLONG, XLONG, GIANT, SINGLE, or DOUBLE.
	The final return value can be extracted from value$$ as follows:
	
	SELECT CASE rtype
	  CASE $$SLONG  : value  = GLOW(value$$)
	  CASE $$XLONG  : value  = value$$
	  CASE $$SINGLE : value! = SMAKE(GLOW(value$$))
	  CASE $$DOUBLE : value# = DMAKE(GHIGH(value$$), GLOW(value$$))
	END SELECT
	
	If specType=-1, rtype!=0, an rtype was returned in value$$,
	but the format is suspect.  Examples include:
	
	0s7F8033jk         ' 8 hex digits required after "0s"
	0d3FED0000000 hi   ' 16 hex digits required after "0d"
	12.34d+8243        ' larger than largest number representable
	
	If specType = SLONG, XLONG, GIANT, SINGLE, or DOUBLE, then
	rtype=specType, and the type was specified in the number.
	Examples of specified numeric types include:
	
	0b1010010010111       ' XLONG:  "0b" that fits in 32-bits
	0b1010...010111       ' XLONG:  "0b" that won't fit in 32-bits
	0o361032723           ' XLONG:  "0o" that fits in 32-bits
	0o7373315631277       ' XLONG:  "0o" that won't fit in 32-bits
	0x12345678            ' XLONG:  "0x" followed by 0-8 hex digits
	0x123456789AB         ' GIANT:  "0x" followed by 9+ hex digits
	0s3F800000            ' SINGLE: "0s" followed by 8 hex digits
	0d3FE0000000000000    ' DOUBLE: "0d" followed by 16 hex digits
	
:Angles
:Degrees
:Radians
#####################
#####  Angles   #####
#####  Degrees  #####
#####  Radians  #####
#####################
Angles are always radians, for both arguments and return values.
	
	To convert degrees to radians, multiply by $$DEGTORAD.
	To convert radians to degrees, multiply by $$RADTODEG.
	
		degrees# = 45
		radians# = degrees# * $$DEGTORAD
		tan#     =  TAN(radians#)         ' tan# = 1
		rad#     = ATAN(tan#)
		deg#     = rad# * $$RADTODEG      ' deg# = 45
	
	See: Math Function Library, Complex Number Function Library
	
:Math Function Library
###################################
#####  Math Function Library  #####
###################################

	IMPORT  "xma"
	
	Angles are always radians.
	
		radian# = 30 * $$DEGTORAD
	
		sin# = SIN(radian#)      'sine
		cos# = COS(radian#)      'cosine
		tan# = TAN(radian#)      'tangent
		cot# = COT(radian#)      'cotangent
		sec# = SEC(radian#)      'secant
		csc# = CSC(radian#)      'cosecant
	
		radian# = ASIN(sin#)     'arc-sine
		radian# = ACOS(cos#)     'arc-cosine
		radian# = ATAN(tan#)     'arc-tangent
		radian# = ACOT(cot#)     'arc-cotangent
		radian# = ASEC(sec#)     'arc-secant
		radian# = ACSC(csc#)     'arc-cosecant
	
		sinh# = SINH(radian#)    'hyperbolic sine
		cosh# = COSH(radian#)    'hyperbolic cosine
		tanh# = TANH(radian#)    'hyperbolic tangent
		coth# = COTH(radian#)    'hyperbolic cotangent
		sech# = SECH(radian#)    'hyperbolic secant
		csch# = CSCH(radian#)    'hyperbolic cosecant
	
		radian# = ASINH(sinh#)   'hyperbolic arc-sine
		radian# = ACOSH(cosh#)   'hyperbolic arc-cosine
		radian# = ATANH(tanh#)   'hyperbolic arc-tangent
		radian# = ACOTH(coth#)   'hyperbolic arc-cotangent
		radian# = ASECH(sech#)   'hyperbolic arc-secant
		radian# = ACSCH(csch#)   'hyperbolic arc-cosecant
	
		degrees# = radian# * $$RADTODEG
	
		log#    = LOG(number#)   'base e logarithm (natural log)
		log10#  = LOG10(number#) 'base 10 logarithm
		number# = EXP(log#)      'base e "anti-log"  (e to the x)
		number# = EXP10(log10#)  'base 10 "anti-log"  (10 to the x)
		sqrt#   = SQRT(number#)  'square root
		number# = sqrt# * sqrt#
		number# = POWER(2, 16)   'power  (x to the y) ' number# = 65536
	
	See: Radians, Complex Number Function Library
	
:Complex Number Function Library
#############################################
#####  Complex Number Function Library  #####
#############################################

	IMPORT  "xcm"
	
	Angles are always radians.
	
	Complex Number Library Functions
	
	SINGLE   = SCABS       (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCACOS      (SCOMPLEX z)
	SINGLE   = SCARG       (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCASIN      (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCATAN      (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCCONJ      (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCCOS       (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCCOSH      (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCEXP       (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCLOG       (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCLOG10     (SCOMPLEX z)
	SINGLE   = SCNORM      (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCPOLAR     (SINGLE magnitude, SINGLE angle)
	SCOMPLEX = SCPOWERCC   (SCOMPLEX z, SCOMPLEX n)
	SCOMPLEX = SCPOWERCR   (SCOMPLEX z, SINGLE n)
	SCOMPLEX = SCPOWERRC   (SINGLE z, SCOMPLEX n)
	SCOMPLEX = SCRMUL      (SCOMPLEX x, SINGLE y)
	SCOMPLEX = SCSIN       (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCSINH      (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCSQRT      (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCTAN       (SCOMPLEX z)
	SCOMPLEX = SCTANH      (SCOMPLEX z)
	
	DOUBLE   = DCABS       (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCACOS      (DCOMPLEX z)
	DOUBLE   = DCARG       (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCASIN      (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCATAN      (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCCONJ      (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCCOS       (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCCOSH      (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCEXP       (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCLOG       (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCLOG10     (DCOMPLEX z)
	DOUBLE   = DCNORM      (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCPOLAR     (DOUBLE magnitude, DOUBLE angle)
	DCOMPLEX = DCPOWERCC   (DCOMPLEX z, DCOMPLEX n)
	DCOMPLEX = DCPOWERCR   (DCOMPLEX z, DOUBLE n)
	DCOMPLEX = DCPOWERRC   (DOUBLE z, DCOMPLEX n)
	DCOMPLEX = DCRMUL      (DCOMPLEX x, DOUBLE y)
	DCOMPLEX = DCSIN       (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCSINH      (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCSQRT      (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCTAN       (DCOMPLEX z)
	DCOMPLEX = DCTANH      (DCOMPLEX z)
	
	See: Radians, Math Function Library
	
:SINGLE Image
##########################
#####  SINGLE Image  #####
##########################

	Single image literals begin with 0s and are followed by
	8 hexadecimal digits. Single image literals are images
	of 32-bit SINGLE data type in 4-bit chunks. Exactly 8
	hex digits must follow the 0s prefix. Any fewer will
	result in an erroneous value, while hex digits beyond 8
	are not included in the single image value.
	
	0s40800000  4.00000
	0s40400000  3.00000
	0s40000000  2.00000
	0s3F800000  1.00000
	0s3F400000  0.75000
	0s3F000000  0.50000
	0s3E800000  0.25000
	0s3E000000  0.12500
	
:DOUBLE Image
##########################
#####  DOUBLE Image  #####
##########################

	Double image literals begin with 0d and are followed by
	16 hexadecimal digits. Double image literals are images
	of 64-bit DOUBLE data type in 4-bit chunks. Exactly 16
	hex digits must follow the 0d prefix. Any fewer will
	result in an erroneous value, while hex digits beyond 16
	are not included in the double image value.
	
	0d4010000000000000  4.00000
	0d4008000000000000  3.00000
	0d4000000000000000  2.00000
	0d3FF0000000000000  1.00000
	0d3FFE800000000000  0.75000
	0d3FE0000000000000  0.50000
	0d3FD0000000000000  0.25000
	0d3FC0000000000000  0.12500
	
:Symbolic Links
:Environment Variables
###################################
#####  Symbolic Links         #####
#####  Environment Variables  #####
###################################

	All XBasic files are installed in the /usr/xb
	directory and the subdirectories of /usr/xb
	
	There should be these symbolic links:
	
	/usr/lib/libxb.a -> /usr/xb/lib/libxb.a
	/usr/bin/xb -> /usr/xb/bin/xb
	
	And these environment variables:
	
	XBDIR="/usr/xb"
	LIB="/usr/X11R6/lib:/usr/xb/lib"
	INCLUDE="/usr/xb/include"
	INSTDIR="/usr/xb"
	
	XBASICBACKUPDIR see notes for "Load Backup" to set this environment variable
	
:Standalone Program
:Assembler,                      main menu > Run
:make Standalone,                main menu > Run
:Execute Standalone,             main menu > Run
#############################################################
#####  Standalone Program                               #####
#####  Assembler,                      main menu > Run  #####
#####  make Standalone,                main menu > Run  #####
#####  Execute Standalone,             main menu > Run  #####
#############################################################

	' Suppose you have a program called "hello.x"
	' that you want to be a standalone executable.
	
	File > Open          'select "hello.x"
	Run > Assembler
	Run > make Standalone
	Run > Execute Standalone
	
	Previous to Xbasic-6.3.13 "make Standalone" and "Execute Standalone"
	were not available and the following procedure was needed:
	
	'	Start a terminal session
	'	Go to the directory with the hello.x file
	'	There should be a files "hello.s" and "hello.mak"
	
	make -f hello.mak
	'	gcc -c -g -ggdb hello.s -o hello.o
	' cc -g -Wall -ggdb hello.o  -rdynamic -L/usr/X11R6/lib -lxb -lX11 -ldl -o hello
	
	./hello
	
	Note:
	By default, standalone programs do not have a console window,
	and console output will be to the the terminal window.
	To have a console requires XstDisplayConsole() at the start
	of the program. And it would probably be a good idea to have
	an INLINE$(prompt$) command just before the program exits,
	so that you have a chance to read what is on the console,
	then press the Enter key before it disappears.
	
	See: XstGetApplicationEnvironment()
	
:Keyboard shortcuts
:Shortcut Keys
:Function Keys (F1-F12)
####################################
#####  Keyboard shortcuts      #####
#####  Shortcut Keys           #####
#####  Function Keys (F1-F12)  #####
####################################

	Insert         - toggles between Insert and Overlay mode
	Ctrl + Insert  - copy selected text into buffer
	Shift + Insert - paste buffered in place of selected text or at cursor position
	Ctrl + Shift + Insert - duplicates (copy and paste)
	
	Ctrl + X  - cut selected textinto buffer
	Ctrl + C  - copy selected text into buffer
	Ctrl + V  - paste buffered in place of selected text or at cursor position
	
	--------------------------------------------------------------------------
	
	The following cursor movement keys will extend the text selection
	if the Shift key is held down at the same time:
	
	LeftArrow         - left one character
	RightArrow        - right one character
	DownArrow         - down one line
	UpArrow           - up one line
	
	Ctrl + LeftArrow  - left to beginning of word
	Ctrl + RightArrow - right to beginning of word
	Ctrl + DownArrow  - bottom of page
	Ctrl + UpArrow    - top of page
	
	Home              - start of line
	End               - end   of line
	Ctrl + Home       - start of text
	Ctrl + End        - end   of text
	
	--------------------------------------------------------------------------
	
	F1 : start program execution from beginning
	F2 : continue program execution after pause
	F3 : pause program execution now
	F4 : kill program execution : continue not possible
	F5 : execute program with breakpoint a cursor line
	F6 : execute single-step local - step over called functions
	F7 : execute single-step global - step into called functions
	F8 : display variables - view and change variables
	F9 : display function call-stack
	F10 : display assembly language for cursor line
	F11 : find string in program or text-file
	F12 : replace string in program or text-file
	
:stop compile on error,          main menu > Options > Misc
########################################################################
#####  stop compile on error,          main menu > Options > Misc  #####
########################################################################

	If this option is checked in Options -> Misc,
	compiling of a program will stop at the completion
	of the function in which the error or errors occurred.
	Only the errors for that function will be displayed.
	All the errors in that function must be cleared before
	it will compile any further.
	
:Breakpoint Trace On,            main menu > Debug
:Breakpoint Trace Off,           main menu > Debug
###############################################################
#####  Breakpoint Trace On,            main menu > Debug  #####
#####  Breakpoint Trace Off,           main menu > Debug  #####
###############################################################

	If Breakpoint Trace is On, each time the program hits a breakpoint
	the frame, line number and function name is printed on the console
	along with the instruction at that location.
	
	If the variables window is being displayed, any variable that has
	changed value since the previous breakpoint, is printed on the
	console plus the (previous value) in parentheses.
	The variable is also highlighted in the variables. If more than one
	variable has changed value, only the first variable is highlighted.
	
